br /> So in this context, its likely that the Cain/Abel story is actually an oral recollection of a real event in that culture's past that has similarly had a morality tale wrapped around it by later generations; basically there's no reason that Biblical Cain and &amp;quot;Blood Caine&amp;quot; (akin to Mitochondrial Eve) couldn't be the same person, but Biblical Cain was only written down after countless generations of oral transmission and so doesn't have everything right... just the gist of &amp;quot;squabbled with and killed his brother then got exiled.&amp;quot; (in WoD lore terms it obviously missed the &amp;quot;he got turned into the first vampire).<br /> <br /> Thus, the way to read both the Book Nod, Lilith's book and the Erycie's Fragments is to take the broad strokes of EVENTS as true, but completely ignore any MOTIVATIONS assigned to any act in the text because those are most likely to have been altered to fit the author's narrative. Ex. Julius Caeser was murdered by members of the Roman Senate is the event... whether this was justified because he was a tyrant, unjustified because he was a populist aiding the common folks and killed by the elites, or just another day of politics where both sides were out for themselves and each side had both selfish and selfless motives for their actions. So I think the broad strokes of Caine is the first, he embraced several 2nd Gens and founded a city. WHY he chose to do so is more nebulous. Similarly, that it was wiped out in a flood seems likely to be real, but it may not have been a result of God's wrath, just unfortunate location relative to rising water.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love this interpretation!<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3598_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3598\" data-vbnamecard=\"3598\">CajunKhan</a> yes, but the Great Flood was a real myth in both Greek and Hebrew tradition. I took this college philosophy class one time (granted, I was twelve at the time and mostly focused on other things, but still) and I specifically remember the professor saying that Plato\u2019s story of Atlantis was based on a real Great Flood myth written down by the poet Hesiod, who lived around the same time as Homer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]}]}